1XXJWS6k-FhsWytVk0N_RRdBt8Bw7SbUuf4TQoN_tnDQENjqsVCxeSQFHzuoWO3rZMbuOFqy4rH=
aL1Ny/4ut/qRzslZSCSR6N7NId8p8i0Q/h30/h001.1WaA-2uOKKsbjQDH2WoMctGiy8_9KgZhc=
ylZm4zWrk4" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">the Nobel Peace Prize</a>, again,</st=
rong> Donald Trump is unlikely to trouble the scorers in Stockholm today wh=
en the economics equivalent is handed out.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Instead, Trump=E2=80=99s Middle Eastern co=
nflict is proving =E2=80=93 to no one=E2=80=99s surprise =E2=80=93 that hig=
h energy prices fuel inflation, and that geopolitical instability hurts gro=
wth. Just this morning, a new report shows that UK firms are facing the sha=
rpest inflation pressures in three years, all down to rising energy, fuel a=
nd raw material costs.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Accountancy and advisory firm BDO has repo=
rted that its inflation index rose to 103.65 in September, up from 102.18 i=
n August and its highest reading since June 2023. These elevated costs and =
weakening demand are putting renewed pressure on firms before the autumn bu=
dget, BDO warns.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Its report has also found that output fell=
 across UK manufacturing and services last month as domestic demand softene=
d.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Scott Knight, head of growth at BDO, says:=
 =E2=80=9CBusinesses head into this budget with one clear message: no surpr=
ises. Cost pressures continue to squeeze margins, hiring intentions are low=
 and growth is starting to plateau.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CFirms are not looking for grand i=
nterventions, but they do need certainty. Right now, concerns about further=
 downside risks outweigh expectations of positive change.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The biggest prize in economics, the Sverig=
es Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel, will be a=
warded today. Known as the <strong>Nobel Prize in Economics</strong> (altho=
ugh it=E2=80=99s not one of the original Nobels), the winner(s) will be ann=
ounced in <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_=
JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IR2EiWaOWoV9d06d4eEaxMnXd4ZjbO7otw-A8ytGNW-JcsxKv63ZuMWIG-8=
UPc0Dozv6F637MhkelAYoWsDxQVKA23OrQ-dxJHM6mHx2Deza00od4dsCC8AlOdNkxOlEJ0mRuN=
hVxjyBaUDbZgjZBT46xU30OMwLwk8b_BtPMAcHaD7nZV3zKqIFu45FoI8sTSySvERo_KQYR6Ijq=
RgmnUMg/4ut/qRzslZSCSR6N7NId8p8i0Q/h31/h001.dpv0qmaCqBGlZRnL6QL6x-Bm6ppL9j5=
yI8QEiUCM9hQ" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">Stockholm</a> from 10.45am UK time.=
</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The prediction markets can give <em>some</=
em> indications about who is under consideration. Leading the betting on <a=
 href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.HT6_61rcAeQ71YM=
HfBFwuSYEcPqRxidXpTUhmbyX9M74jLM2j5GXG70b-5aIGfJFtkjFHVtefRzdwPWV5C7wWVFaKA=
QYei04OlnY7ZnYF_uOokiZdC498mXyh6bROQ6hBIU5hZp3CaDqNmqbjQ0qqT2O7B-0SjK6b1GoO=
RBAfHDCFExiIewxspy1n-9H2irPob-sCtEhKT95SFIE-5768viPmZFc1vKfTWrovO40I29WrB6a=
_-vNKGISNxg5Q9Df/4ut/qRzslZSCSR6N7NId8p8i0Q/h32/h001.6v0GfiSGiWt2oH55Ju2BrR=
m3NKG2_q2cA7BEa62LkpM" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">Polymarket</a>, at 30%, is=
 David Autor, professor of economics at the Massachusetts Institute of Tech=
nology. Autor is a labour economist, who has examined how technology change=
s the world of work, and <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.co=
m/ss/c/u001.8P_iYkg0_u5AycVUkiWn7ClFbYwdAod_vZlfj3D_672_uJJ3Inm54yIW4GGtTn0=
nIZYB8mHSaiM0dDG3B-hNrVRIyaBw_Iph66YErOiBd6H1egLftrwxxFGLsWr0Xs16OqwVvsElqs=
6q0vgl-xZSH7akBJQaMMOgM3Gty3tRdzkx9_jwbSiGX8fmnbAd3tS-RFmaNehz-Bjxz1OdnOKe3=
Icji7KWAIjWxjj7cfQjdYSsSwK70m-8OhkZ4i1S9QHjUtHiOFzySuPe0fNVhgHfvKeQZ-4io2r7=
9Bf4J4siXtFzDpiO1mLKMtpg8yBdLdwP/4ut/qRzslZSCSR6N7NId8p8i0Q/h33/h001.S7qAyS=
R5CRoKrkBX8I4bU5TiQTp_CA1LTaD3E7SolRs" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">shown</a> =
how computerisation has increased skill-based wage premia in the US.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Another potential winner is Ari=C3=A9l Pak=
es, the Thomas Professor of <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian=
.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEDFD-3QXV9s0oG0hIOZ=
kea-xjqiIYxeMLGBMeZipTyvEPE36JTQpOrRm_NO_Lv_fbK9d1vRlQxiFTLvusBhLnKCgWpE74A=
whEBL48QSepg2YSouAK7d2hSvruOiGxptDB1O-_RDkvu3-ukV6wJBb5VaTS14sGt_BuBBbmFjhp=
urAlK5Sv3k3t_uFIPEhxyzaTs/4ut/qRzslZSCSR6N7NId8p8i0Q/h34/h001.1x7MIV4ZKcg7M=
hASENOFfxmJmWHxn-mlznbnkjOAElg" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">Economics</a> at =
Harvard University, with a 19% chance on Polymarket. Pakes has focused on d=
eveloping methods to analyse market responses to environmental and policy c=
hanges, such as mergers and deregulation.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Third, at 12%, is Susan Athey, the Economi=
cs of Technology Professor at Stanford Graduate School of Business. <strong=
>Athey</strong> =E2=80=93 whose work has focused on auctions, by developing=
 the empirical models which underpin them =E2=80=93 is the former consultin=
g chief economist for <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/s=
s/c/u001.8P_iYkg0_u5AycVUkiWn7G2NbW-SadK6MiFfsuSK2bAYJvc8Z2hUvgaDcsU3fpEKfy=
bC75n4OyUoP8rDqTqzgZ0E0mo0Gqx7B0-oq_GE73jQvWZrRMfnGcP-gMoG_9mcQZCyzJ2Wh-et6=
2Mz-YwuXJ9cuJI7vLhSbujnY-Ofo5KDbkGmLgJJ4V4EdU9NT8Q_wxPONrQvvBuXtEApngDdCj85=
cOb3iVqr3VPuJKZhpug/4ut/qRzslZSCSR6N7NId8p8i0Q/h35/h001.vEuVxhV4u7-NfqQ07Ia=
Ml5VTjPRnH2zHtb3suK1rguw" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">Microsoft</a>.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">There=E2=80=99s also interest in American =
macroeconomist and monetary theorist <strong>Michael</strong> <strong>Woodf=
ord</strong> (10%) =E2=80=93 he=E2=80=99s one of the architects of modern f=
orward guidance, the idea that central banks can control the economy throug=
h influencing public expectations about inflation, rather than just through=
 interest rates.</p>
